        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-group" id="domain">
      <h2 class="faq-group-title">Domain, email, and ownership</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Who owns my domain? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">You do, and you always will. Your domain is registered in your name, at your registrar, and you control it. I point it at my hosting through its settings (DNS), and if you've lost track of where it's registered, I'll help you find it.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Do you handle email on my domain? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">I don't host email, but I set up the records that make it work (MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC) for whichever provider you choose: Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, or simple forwarding to your current inbox. That setup is part of launch, at no extra charge.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What happens to my domain if I lose track of the renewal? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">I track your domain's expiration date (WHOIS) myself, since registrar reminders often land in spam, and I remind you 60 days, 30 days, and one week ahead. If a domain does lapse, most endings (such as .com) have a 30-day recovery window, and I can help you get it back.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">If I cancel, can I take my site elsewhere? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Your domain, yes: I help you move it to a registrar you choose. The website goes offline at the end of your term, because I own the code on the monthly plan. There's no export of the site and no code buyout. Your content and forms account stay yours, and there's no exit fee.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-group" id="privacy">
      <h2 class="faq-group-title">Privacy and data</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Will my site need a cookie banner? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No. The fonts and visitor statistics I use set no cookies and meet GDPR, Europe's privacy law. I add a banner only if you choose a tracker that needs one, such as Google Analytics or the Facebook Pixel. I usually advise against those, because they slow the site down and most browsers block them anyway.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Where do form submissions go? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Form messages go straight to your business email through <span class="term" data-term="An outside service that delivers form messages to your email without needing a server.">a form-delivery service</span>, and I never store a copy. I see your messages only if you forward one to me.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Do you sell data, share with ad networks, or use trackers? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No to all three. The <a href="/privacy-policy/" class="inline-link">privacy policy</a> has the details, and the whole setup rests on one principle: collect only the personal data I need.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-group" id="maintenance">
      <h2 class="faq-group-title">Maintenance and the long term</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">How do I know if a request is an update or a new feature? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Editing the pages you have, such as text, photos, hours, or prices, is a content update and is included. An extra page beyond the 10 included costs $100 per page. New behavior, such as a calculator, a gallery, or an online store, is a feature, and I quote it first. Either way, I tell you in advance what's included and what's billable.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What happens if the hosting network has an outage? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Most outages on the hosting network are regional, so visitors elsewhere still see your site. A global outage makes it unreachable until the outage ends, and I watch the network's status reports and post updates. In the past 12 months on this setup, I've seen one global incident: it lasted 27 minutes and affected about 4 percent of global traffic.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What's in the Owner's Guide? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Its 17 sections cover life after launch, from who handles what and how to ask for changes to your domain, email, hosting, security, analytics, invoices, and leaving if it ever comes to that. You can read the full <a href="/owners-guide/" class="inline-link">Owner's Guide</a> at any time.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">How often does the code behind my site get updated? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Your site is custom code, with nothing like a plug-in or an admin panel to patch, so the site itself never needs patching. Every quarter, I review its build tools and security settings, and the hosting network patches its own systems. You'll never get an urgent "your CMS needs a security update" email.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if my business name or branding changes? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Your site can take a rebrand without a rebuild. On the monthly plan, a new name and logo with new fonts and colors takes about a day and counts as a content update. A full redesign is a separate project, quoted like a new build. Either way, redirects from the old name go in on launch day, so your search rankings carry over.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

      </article>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

